## 2. OWASP Top‑10 mapping

| OWASP Top‑10 Category | Why it matters for this app | Current state | Recommendation |
|-----------------------|-----------------------------|--------------|----------------|
| A1: Injection | Injection only occurs when user input flows to DB/command. | No user input; safe for now. | Add defensive coding and validate any future inputs. |
| A2: Broken Authentication | No auth/authorization; all endpoints are publicly reachable. | Present | Add session, JWT, or API key auth; enforce “least privilege.” |
| A3: Sensitive Data Exposure | No data is sensitive, but you’ll store user data eventually. | Safe now. | Encrypt credentials at rest; use HTTPS for transport. |
| A4: XML External Entities (XXE) | No XML parsing. | Safe. | N/A. |
| A5: Broken Access Control | Anything after you expose the `/users` route is visible to anyone. | Present | Use role‑based access control; limit `/users` to admins. |
| A6: Security Misconfiguration | Default Express settings expose helpful headers (`X-Powered-By`), no CSP, no helmet. | Present | Harden headers; use `helmet()`; disable production mode options. |
| A7: Cross‑Site Scripting (XSS) | Response data is static JSON; no HTML rendering. | Safe. | If you ever surface user data in a browser, encode/escape. |
| A8: Insecure Deserialization | No serialization. | Safe. | N/A. |
| A9: Using Components With Known Vulnerabilities | Express 4 (latest) is fine, but dependencies may drift. | Unknown yet. | Keep `package.json` up‑to‑date, use `npm audit`. |
