## 4. Recommendations & Code Samples

Below are code snippets you can drop straight into `app.js` (or into a separate `middleware.js` file) to close the gaps. Feel free to refactor into a dedicated configuration file.

### 4.1 Add Security Headers (Helmet)

```js
const helmet = require('helmet');

// Add helmet *before* route definitions
app.use(helmet());
```

*Benefits:* sets `Strict-Transport-Security`, `X-Content-Type-Options`, `X-Frame-Options`, etc.

---

### 4.2 Request Logging (Morgan)

```js
const morgan = require('morgan');

// Use the "combined" format for server‑side logs
app.use(morgan('combined'));
```

*Benefits:* HTTP logs are essential for troubleshooting and for detecting abusive traffic.

---

### 4.3 Rate Limiting (express-rate-limit)

```js
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15‑minute window
  max: 200,                 // limit each IP to 200 requests per window
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
});

// Apply globally or only to certain routes
app.use('/health', limiter);
```

*Benefits:* protects against trivial DoS and scanning.

---

### 4.4 CORS Policy

```js
const cors = require('cors');

// Allow only trusted origins (replace with your real domain)
app.use(cors({
  origin: 'https://mytrustedapp.com',
  optionsSuccessStatus: 200,
}));
```

If the API is meant to be public, you can use `{ origin: true }` but still restrict to a whitelist in production.

---

### 4.5 Basic Error Handling

```js
// 404 handler (must be defined *after* routes)
app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not Found' });
});

// Generic error handler (catch all)
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {   // eslint-disable-line no-unused-vars
  console.error(err.stack);          // Log stack trace
  res.status(err.status || 500).json({
    error: err.message || 'Internal Server Error',
  });
});
```

*Benefits:* prevents stack traces leaking to clients and provides graceful responses.

---

### 4.6 Authentication Stub (JWT)

Even if `/users` is read‑only today, you’ll likely want an auth layer later:

```js
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
const secret = process.env.JWT_SECRET || 'change-me';

function verifyToken(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];
  if (!token) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Missing token' });

  jwt.verify(token, secret, (err, decoded) => {
    if (err) return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
    req.user = decoded; // attach decoded payload
    next();
  });
}
```

> Add `verifyToken` as middleware on any route that needs protection:

```js
app.get('/users', verifyToken, (req, res) => { … });
```

---

### 4.7 Input Validation (express-validator)

If future endpoints accept query or JSON payloads:

```js
const { body, query, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

app.post('/user',
  body('email').isEmail(),
  body('name').isLength({ min: 1, max: 50 }),
  (req, res) => {
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty())
      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });

    // Proceed with logic
  });
```

---

### 4.8 Use Environment Variables

Move configuration out of the code:

```js
app.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${process.env.PORT || 3000}`);
});
```

Set `PORT` & `JWT_SECRET` via `.env` (use `dotenv` for dev).

---

### 4.9 Dependency Auditing (practical tip)

```bash
# In the workspace directory
npm audit   # or yarn audit
```

Keep Express, Helmet, Morgan, etc. on safe versions (`"express": "^4.18.2"`).

