### 3.1. Rate Limiting (A6 – Security Misconfiguration)

A public health or users endpoint can be DDoS‑attacked or abused for scraping. Add a light‐weight rate limiter.

```js
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

// 100 requests / 15 minutes per IP
const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs   : 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max        : 100,
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
  message     : { error: 'Too many requests, please try again later.' }
});

app.use(limiter);  // Apply to all routes
```

> **Tip** – If you only want to protect some endpoints, mount the middleware on a router or particular route instead of the whole app.

---

### 3.2. Security Headers (A6 – Security Misconfiguration)

Inject a minimal set of HTTP security headers using `helmet`.

```js
const helmet = require('helmet');
app.use(helmet());  // Sets many headers by default
```

> For development, `helmet()` is usually enough. In production you might also add a CSP that restricts content to the same origin.

---

### 3.3. Environment‑Aware Error Handling

Make sure that stack traces are never leaked to the outside world. In development you want detailed errors, in production you want generic answers.

```js
// Very simple global error handler
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production') {
    return res.status(500).json({ error: err.message, stack: err.stack });
  }
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
});
```

> Ensure `NODE_ENV` is set to `production` when you deploy.

---

### 3.4. Content-Type & JSON Schema Validation

If you later add POST/PUT routes that consume user input, validate payloads with a validator library like `Joi` or `express-validator`.  

```js
const { body, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

app.post('/users',
  body('name').isString().trim().notEmpty(),
  body('email').isEmail(),
  (req, res) => {
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) { return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() }); }
    // TODO: store user – use parameter binding / prepared statements to avoid injection
    res.status(201).json({ message: 'User created' });
  });
```

> Validation protects against injection (A1) and ensures the data you work with meets expectations.

---

### 3.5. Sensitive Data Exposure – Health Endpoint

If you care about information disclosure, remove the exact timestamp or mask it:

```js
app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok' }); // no timestamp
});
```

> Exposing the server time can aid a timing‑based attack or fingerprinting.

---

### 3.6. Access Control (A2: Broken Authentication, A5: Broken Access Control)

Even for a “demo” you might wish to protect the `/users` endpoint:

```js
// Dummy authentication middleware
function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  const auth = req.headers.authorization;
  if (!auth || auth !== 'Bearer mysecrettoken') {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Unauthorized' });
  }
  next();
}

// Apply to the users route
app.get('/users', requireAuth, (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
